From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] SPU: an interpreted indirect callee stops at its own return point

Upstream: not yet submitted; written to be upstreamed (generic, no title names)
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0039)

What: a lifted indirect call (bisl) now goes through spu_indirect_call(ctx,
return_pc), which makes return_pc the innermost return point (drain_ret_pc)
while the callee is dispatched, then drains to it as before. A callee with
no lifted code is run by the interpreter inside that dispatch, before
spu_drain_call has set drain_ret_pc, and the interpreter stops at
drain_ret_pc. It used to see the PREVIOUS call's return point, so it ran on
past its own return through the caller's code until the next lifted entry;
the caller's later returns then met drain loops waiting for other return
points (each one a restart at depth 0), and a SPURS policy module's run could
end in the middle of a job, leaving the job's pipeline record in flight for
ever (a job queue's fence that never passes). Seen with a sort job whose
comparator, reached only through a function pointer, has no lifted code.
The lifter emits the new call; its standalone selftest and the interpreter
diff test get the matching stub.
Behaviour reference: SPU ISA (bisl: link, then branch; the callee returns
through the link); no emulator code.
Tests: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_interpreter_handoff.c, new mode
nested-return-indirect-call (an unlifted callee dispatched at once inside the
call bracket, returning to an unlifted return point: the interpreter stops
there and the host frame continues; with the old bracket the interpreter
runs on into the caller's code and the check fails).
diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_drain.c b/runtime/spu/spu_drain.c
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_drain.c
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_drain.c
@@ -628,3 +628,20 @@ void spu_drain_call(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t return_pc)
     }
 #undef SPU_DRAIN_POP_IRQ
 }
+
+/* A lifted indirect call (bisl): dispatch the callee, then drain until it
+ * returns to return_pc. While the callee is dispatched, return_pc is already
+ * the innermost return point: a callee with no lifted code runs in the
+ * interpreter inside spu_indirect_branch, before spu_drain_call has set it,
+ * and stops there (spu_smc_microstep). Before, it stopped at the previous
+ * call's return point, so it ran on past its own return through the caller's
+ * code to the next lifted entry, and the caller's later returns met drain
+ * loops waiting for other return points. */
+void spu_indirect_call(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t return_pc)
+{
+    const uint32_t outer_ret_pc = ctx->drain_ret_pc;
+    ctx->drain_ret_pc = return_pc & SPU_LS_MASK;
+    spu_indirect_branch(ctx);
+    ctx->drain_ret_pc = outer_ret_pc;   /* spu_drain_call keeps the outer one for its exit */
+    spu_drain_call(ctx, return_pc);
+}
diff --git a/runtime/spu/spu_context.h b/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
--- a/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
+++ b/runtime/spu/spu_context.h
@@ -923,6 +923,10 @@ void spu_irqwait_diag(spu_context* ctx);
 
 /* Drain a translated call up to its explicit architectural return PC. */
 void spu_drain_call(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t return_pc);
+/* A lifted indirect call (bisl): spu_indirect_branch to ctx->pc with return_pc
+ * already the innermost return point (an interpreted callee stops there), then
+ * spu_drain_call(ctx, return_pc). spu_drain.c. */
+void spu_indirect_call(spu_context* ctx, uint32_t return_pc);
 /* SPU_PCHIST=1: bucket SPU execution by the GPU module's own symbols.
  *
  * SPU_VRAMPC showed 3.7M VRAM writes from Host2Local_Body and ZERO from
